      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "InSapphoWeTrust from Los Angeles, California, USA",
      "description": "I am taking a tour that allows me to see some facilities of the Kennedy Space Center up close.\nThis will be as close as I will ever get to a launch pad, however. (It is way too dangerous out there honestly.)\nThe gravel road in front is used to transport the Space Shuttle stack from the Vehicle Assembly Building (near me) to the launchpads.\n\nIn the distance is one of the two launchpads, that have served the Apollo program and the Space Shuttle program. This particular one in sight is 39B. Its first use for the Space Shuttle was for Mission 51-L on 28 January 1986, the tenth flight for the shuttle Challenger. Sadly that flight ended in an explosion 73 seconds after launch, killing the crew of seven.",
